NAME
       seq - print a sequence of numbers

SYNOPSIS
       seq [-s SEPARATOR] [-w] [-f FORMAT] [FIRST [STEP]] LAST

DESCRIPTION
       seq prints the numbers from FIRST (default 1) to LAST, adding STEP
       (default 1) each time, one per line. It is handy for loops in
       scripts: for i in $(seq 1 5). In bash, {1..5} does the same.

OPTIONS
       -s, --separator TEXT
              Put TEXT between the numbers instead of a newline.

       -w, --equal-width
              Pad the numbers with leading zeros to the same width.

       -f, --format FORMAT
              printf-style format, such as -f 'user%02g'.

EXAMPLES
           seq 5
           seq 10 -2 0
           seq -w 1 10
           for n in $(seq 1 3); do useradd user$n; done

SEE ALSO
       bash(1), printf(1), shuf(1)
